## 2. The Confusion Matrix — Foundation of Everything Else

Every metric in this topic is CALCULATED from the **Confusion Matrix** — understand this first, everything else follows naturally.

```
                    Predicted: No    Predicted: Yes
Actual: No          TN (True Neg)    FP (False Pos)
Actual: Yes          FN (False Neg)   TP (True Pos)
```

| Term | Meaning |
|------|---------|
| **TP** (True Positive) | Model said "Yes", and it WAS actually Yes — correct! |
| **TN** (True Negative) | Model said "No", and it WAS actually No — correct! |
| **FP** (False Positive) | Model said "Yes", but it was actually No — a mistake ("false alarm") |
| **FN** (False Negative) | Model said "No", but it was actually Yes — a mistake ("missed it") |

**Syntax:**
```python
from sklearn.metrics import confusion_matrix

cm = confusion_matrix(y_test, y_pred)
```

```python
from sklearn.metrics import confusion_matrix

y_test = [1, 0, 1, 1, 0, 0, 1, 0]
y_pred = [1, 0, 0, 1, 0, 1, 1, 0]

cm = confusion_matrix(y_test, y_pred)
print(cm)
# [[3 1]
#  [1 3]]
# Row 0 = Actual "0" (3 correctly predicted as 0, 1 wrongly predicted as 1)
# Row 1 = Actual "1" (1 wrongly predicted as 0, 3 correctly predicted as 1)
```

### Visualizing it (using Seaborn — you already know this!)

```python
import seaborn as sns
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt
from sklearn.metrics import confusion_matrix

cm = confusion_matrix(y_test, y_pred)

sns.heatmap(cm, annot=True, cmap="Blues", fmt="d",
            xticklabels=["Predicted No", "Predicted Yes"],
            yticklabels=["Actual No", "Actual Yes"])
plt.title("Confusion Matrix")
plt.show()
```

